Yes—with an important precision: **40 hours per week establishes substantial commitment, not vocational mastery by itself**. The strongest application will describe the campaign as **full-time-equivalent, self-directed vocational development** and demonstrate mastery through Julian’s responsibilities, work products, longevity, and measurable effects on participants.

### Portfolio-ready framing

> **Self-Directed Vocational Development in Collaborative Narrative and Project Leadership**  
>  
> Julian dedicates approximately 40 hours each week to designing, administering, and facilitating a long-running collaborative roleplaying campaign. This full-time-equivalent commitment extends well beyond recreational participation. He manages an evolving system of characters, rules, timelines, player objectives, and interconnected narratives while coordinating the contributions and expectations of multiple participants.  
>  
> His work demonstrates advanced project-management practices: long-range planning, session preparation, documentation, scheduling, scope management, continuity control, rapid adaptation, and iterative improvement based on participant feedback. As facilitator, Julian also exercises leadership by maintaining engagement, balancing competing creative priorities, resolving interpersonal and in-game conflicts, and creating structures through which other participants can contribute meaningfully.  
>  
> The campaign functions as a sustained, self-directed practicum in narrative systems design, group facilitation, and creative production. Julian is not merely learning isolated skills or showing future potential; he is repeatedly applying an integrated skill set to operate and improve a complex collaborative project.

### Stronger mastery claim—if the portfolio documents it

> Julian has demonstrated vocational-level mastery in the operation of a complex collaborative narrative project. Over **[duration]**, he has coordinated **[number] participants**, managed **[number] characters or story arcs**, produced **[specific artifacts]**, and facilitated approximately **[number] sessions**. The project’s continuity, participant retention, and capacity to incorporate consequential player decisions provide concrete evidence of his proficiency in systems design, project administration, and collaborative leadership.

For credibility, attach evidence such as:

- campaign timelines, planning documents, maps, databases, or “story bibles”;
- calendars, session agendas, recaps, and version histories;
- participant numbers, retention, session frequency, and project duration;
- examples of redesigns made after feedback;
- a case study of a scheduling, scope, or interpersonal conflict he resolved;
- short participant testimonials describing his reliability and leadership;
- original mechanics, onboarding materials, or accessibility tools he created.

One wording distinction will help with admissions: call it a **“full-time-equivalent commitment”** rather than literally a “full-time job” unless Julian has formal employment, clients, or compensation. Likewise, separate hours spent actively managing and designing the campaign from hours spent solely playing. That precision makes the mastery argument more persuasive, not less.
